WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.237
Choose by measuring.

2
00:00:01.517 --> 00:00:04.099
Every claim in this lesson came with “on their data.

3
00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:13.061
” The way to choose for yours is a small retrieval evaluation, and it is the same tool you will use for every retrieval change in this studio.

4
00:00:13.341 --> 00:00:22.387
Build a set of 50 to 100 questions that real users would ask, each labeled with the passage (document plus location) that answers it.

5
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:41.120
Start from real queries if you have logs; otherwise write them with a domain expert, or have a model draft questions from sampled passages and have a person keep only the realistic ones (the Chroma report’s machine-generated questions are a reminder that generated sets carry the generator’s style).

6
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:49.699
Then hold everything else fixed (embedding model, k, the query side) and vary one thing at a time: strategy, chunk size, overlap.

7
00:00:49.979 --> 00:01:02.395
Score each configuration by whether the gold passage appears in the top k, using recall@k and MRR from week 1, and record the index size and indexing cost beside the scores.

8
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:04.680
Two refinements matter in practice.

9
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:16.757
Define a hit by overlap with the gold location, not by chunk id, because different strategies produce different chunk ids for the same text; your character offsets make this easy.

10
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:26.765
And look at the failures, not just the averages: a strategy that wins on average but loses every table question may be the wrong choice for a finance team.

11
00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:31.120
Finally, choose the simplest configuration within noise of the best.

12
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:42.771
If structure-aware chunking at 400 characters scores the same as semantic chunking at three times the indexing cost, ship the cheaper one and keep the eval set for the next change.
